Part 1
Predicting Customer Churn
Overview
This report outlines the definition, data, model development, and evaluation/deployment plan
for a hypothetical AI project focused on predicting customer churn for a subscription-based
software service (SaaS).

1. Problem Definition
Hypothetical AI Problem
Predicting which existing customers are likely to cancel their subscription (churn) within the
next 30 days.

Objectives
1. Retention: Accurately identify high-risk customers to enable proactive intervention
by the sales and customer success teams.
2. Resource Allocation: Optimize the allocation of customer success resources by
focusing efforts on the most salvageable customers.
3. Profitability: Minimize revenue loss associated with customer attrition.

Stakeholders
1. Customer Success/Sales Team: They will use the predictions to prioritize outreach
and retention strategies.
2. Executive Leadership/Finance: They are accountable for overall company growth
and revenue targets, directly impacted by churn rates.

Key Performance Indicator (KPI)
Reduction in Customer Churn Rate: The ultimate business measure of success will be a
quantifiable decrease (e.g., 5% year-over-year) in the actual customer churn rate within the
segments targeted by the prediction model.

2. Data Collection & Preprocessing
Data Sources
1. Product Usage Database: Contains usage patterns, such as login frequency,
feature adoption rate, number of support tickets filed, and time since last active
session.
2. Customer Relationship Management (CRM) System: Contains customer
demographics, subscription details (plan type, contract length, billing history), and
historical customer support interaction logs.

Potential Bias
Historical Intervention Bias: The historical data may be biased because past customer
success teams have already intervened with some at-risk customers, potentially masking
their true likelihood of churning. For example, a customer who was truly high-risk might be
labeled as "retained" only because of an aggressive discount, making the model learn that
all customers with their profile are low-risk, when they are not.

Preprocessing Steps
1. Handling Missing Data: Use mean/median imputation for missing numerical
usage statistics and mode imputation for missing categorical data (e.g., industry
type).
2. Feature Engineering: Create new, predictive features, such as "Time Since Last
Login" (days) and "Rate of Feature Usage Change" (current month vs. previous 3
months).
3. Categorical Encoding: Convert categorical variables (e.g., subscription plan,
industry, region) into a numerical format suitable for modeling, such as One-Hot
Encoding for nominal variables.

3. Model Development
Model Choice and Justification
XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting):
Justification: XGBoost is an ensemble method based on decision trees, known for its high
predictive power and ability to handle a mix of numerical and categorical data with complex,
non-linear relationships. It performs well on imbalanced classification problems (churn is
typically rare) and provides feature importance scores, which are valuable for the customer
success team to understand why a customer is flagged as high-risk.

Data Split
The data would be split chronologically to ensure the model is trained on past data and
evaluated on future data, mirroring the real-world deployment scenario.
● Training Set (70%): Data from the earliest time period (e.g., 2 years ago up to 3
months ago) used to train the model.
● Validation Set (15%): Data from a more recent period (e.g., the period between 3
months ago and 1 month ago) used for hyperparameter tuning and early stopping.
● Test Set (15%): Data from the most recent period (e.g., the last month) used for a
final, unbiased evaluation of the model's performance before deployment.

Hyperparameters to Tune
1. n_estimators (Number of Trees): Tuning this prevents overfitting by finding the
optimal number of sequential models (trees) to add to the ensemble before
performance on the validation set starts to degrade.
2. max_depth (Maximum Tree Depth): Tuning this controls the complexity of the
individual trees. A lower depth simplifies the model and prevents it from learning
highly specific patterns in the training data (i.e., reduces overfitting).

4. Evaluation & Deployment
Evaluation Metrics and Relevance
1.F1-Score: This is the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall. It is critical because
churn is an imbalanced classification problem (most customers don't churn). The
F1-Score provides a balanced measure of performance, ensuring the model doesn't
simply predict "no churn" for all instances (high accuracy, low actual usefulness).

2.Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC): This
measures the model's ability to discriminate between the two classes (churn vs. no
churn). It's relevant because it evaluates performance across all possible
classification thresholds, allowing the business to choose a threshold that
balances the cost of false positives (wasting retention resources) versus the cost of
false negatives (losing a customer).

Concept Drift and Monitoring
Concept Drift occurs when the statistical properties of the target variable (customer churn
behavior) change over time in ways that the model did not anticipate during training. For
example, a new competitor or a change in pricing structure could suddenly change the
predictors that lead to churn.

Monitoring Post-Deployment
Concept drift would be monitored by periodically comparing the model's predicted churn
probability distribution on new data to the distribution observed during the test phase. More
formally, we would track the model's performance metrics (e.g., F1-Score) on the live,
incoming data, using a sliding window. If the performance on the live data drops significantly
(e.g., more than 5% over a month) and consistently, it indicates that the underlying customer
behavior has changed, necessitating model retraining.

Technical Challenge During Deployment
Real-Time Latency and Scalability: The system must generate predictions quickly (low
latency) for a massive, growing number of active customers and serve those predictions to
the CRM or customer success dashboard in real-time. This requires a scalable,
high-throughput serving infrastructure (e.g., an API endpoint backed by a containerized
service like Kubernetes) to handle concurrent requests without system failure or significant
delay, which would render the "proactive" intervention useless.



